{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Acting on behalf of{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Acting on behalf of — the actor behind a requester</h1>
    <p>
        The component distinguishes <em>who access is asked for</em> (the requester) from
        <em>who is really asking</em> (the actor), the way RFC&nbsp;8693 does. Two shapes carry it:
        Security's <code>SwitchUserToken</code> (impersonation), and the component-native
        <code>DelegatedRequesterInterface</code> — used here by <code>OnBehalfOfSession</code>,
        with no firewall or <code>switch_user</code> involved.
    </p>
    <p>
        Both rows below are decided <strong>programmatically</strong> for an explicit requester
        (<code>AccessInspector::isGrantedFor()</code>), so nothing depends on who is logged in.
        The agent behind the second row is an administrator, yet the session it drives still holds
        only the customer's <code>ROLE_USER</code>.
    </p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Scenario</th>
                <th>Real actor</th>
                {% for attribute, question in questions %}
                    <th title="{{ question }}"><code>{{ attribute }}</code></th>
                {% endfor %}
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in rows %}
                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <strong>{{ row.title }}</strong><br>
                        <span class="muted">{{ row.description }}</span>
                    </td>
                    <td>{% if row.actor %}<code>{{ row.actor }}</code>{% else %}<span class="muted">—</span>{% endif %}</td>
                    {% for attribute, question in questions %}
                        <td>{{ row.checks[attribute] ? '✓ granted' : '✗ denied' }}</td>
                    {% endfor %}
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="muted">
        Read the last column together with the first: <code>IS_ACTING_ADMIN</code> is granted by an
        app voter that reads the <em>actor's</em> roles via <code>Actor::of()</code>, so a break-glass
        action can be allowed on “an admin is really behind this” without ever widening the customer's
        own <code>ROLE_ADMIN</code>, which stays denied.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
